---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { implementedBodies } from '../lib/bodies';
import { normalizeBase } from '../lib/base';
import { repoUrl } from '../lib/site';
const base = normalizeBase(import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
---
<BaseLayout title="Body catalog">
  <section class="hero page-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Catalog state</p>
      <h1>Stable IDs prevent duplicate knowledge.</h1>
      <p class="lede">The simulator gives each current body a stable <code>BodyId</code> and parent relationship. Renderer colors and moon separation derive from that metadata rather than body-name matching.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button-secondary" href={`${base}docs/roadmap/`}>Read body-by-body roadmap</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel table-wrap science-table">
    <table aria-label="Implemented body initialization catalog">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Body</th><th>Kind</th><th>Parent</th><th>Milestone</th><th>Initialization note</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {implementedBodies.map((body) => (
          <tr id={body.slug}>
            <td data-label="Body">{body.name}</td>
            <td data-label="Kind">{body.kind}</td>
            <td data-label="Parent">{body.parent}</td>
            <td data-label="Milestone">{body.milestone}</td>
             <td data-label="Initialization note">{body.initialization}{body.group && <p>{body.summary}</p>}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Data provenance</h2>
    <p>The table above is the 128-body core scene. The <a href={`${base}small-bodies/`}>complete small-body atlas</a> separately exposes 1,564,244 JPL catalog entries through bounded search and orbital previews, with explicit selected-body experiments.</p>
    <p>The 115 Jovian moons use <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/elem/">JPL mean orbital elements</a> and the <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/data/jovian_moons.json`}>versioned satellite snapshot</a>. Mass is derived from published GM / G. Missing physical values remain Unknown; 106 moons are modeled as massless test particles with nonphysical wire markers. Published estimates are labeled. These are representative orbital shapes and orientations, not dated ephemeris states.</p>
    <p>Current constants are educational baseline values, not epoch state vectors. Jupiter and Saturn use <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html">JPL physical parameters</a> and <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html">JPL approximate orbital elements</a>. Saturn's visible ring extent and tilt follow <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/">NASA's Saturn facts</a>, but the rings remain renderer-only. Satellite values use <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/">JPL Solar System Dynamics physical parameters</a>; Vesta uses <a href="https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/sbdb.api?sstr=4%20Vesta&phys-par=1&full-prec=1">JPL SBDB solution 36</a>. Derived periapsis distances and vis-viva speeds remain in <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/src/sim/constants.h`}>the source constants</a>.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
